14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240907 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240907 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240907 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240907 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ for $x\\in [1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240907 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45e3e133-a99c-58ca-b74f-4cbfe235bb72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240907 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2)$ and $(2,9).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(2,9)-f(1,2) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot 1-{e}^{1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
